N.B.: To develop a customized WordPress plugin, you need to have expertise in WordPress development, PHP, HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

To create a user-friendly WordPress plugin that adds customized functionality to an existing website, follow the below steps:

1. Analyze the Website: Analyze the existing website's structure, design, branding, and user behavior, and identify the features that need plugin integration.

2. Plan the Plugin's Features: Based on the website requirements, plan the plugin's features, including a new custom post type (CPT), a new widget, and additional shortcodes.

3. Create a Unique Plugin Name and Short Description: Choose a unique name and short description that reflects the plugin's functionality, branded according to the website's aesthetic.

4. Set Up the Plugin: Create a new folder for the plugin and initialize a new WordPress plugin using the WordPress plugin boilerplate.

   ```php
   <?php
   /**
   * Plugin Name: [Unique Plugin Name]
   * Plugin URI: [Plugin URL]
   * Description: [Short Description: Quickly summarize plugin's functionality and features]
   * Version: [Your Version]
   * Author: [Your Name]
   * Author URI: [Your Website URL]
   * Text Domain: [Unique Plugin Name]
   * Domain Path: /languages
   */
   defined( 'ABSPATH' ) || exit;

   // Add your plugin functionality here
   ```

5. Create Custom Post Type: Add a new custom post type (CPT) to the plugin to create a unique type of content for the website.

   ```php
   add_action( 'init', 'register_custom_post_type', 0 );

   function register_custom_post_type() {
      $args = array(
         'public' => true,
         'label' => 'Custom Post Type',
         'menu_icon' => 'dashicons-welcome-write-blog',
         'supports' => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
      );
      register_post_type( 'custom_post_type', $args );
   }
   ```

6. Add a Widget: Create a new widget for the website's sidebars or other widgetized areas.

   ```php
   class My_Widget extends WP_Widget {
      function __construct() {
         parent::__construct(
             'my_widget',
             __( 'My Widget', 'textdomain' ),
             array( 'description' => __( 'Add My Widget', 'textdomain' ) )
         );
      }

      function widget( $args, $instance ) {
         // Display widget content here
      }

      // add other functions if required
   }
   register_widget( 'My_Widget' );
   ```

7. Add Shortcodes: Implement custom shortcodes to enhance content creation, with attributes for custom options.

   ```php
   function my_shortcode( $atts, $content = null ) {
      extract( shortcode_atts( array(
         'attribute1' => 'default value',
         'attribute2' => true,
      ), $atts, 'my_shortcode' ) );

      // Return shortcode content using attributes
      return $content . '<div>Attribute 1: ' . $attribute1 . '</div>';
   }

   add_shortcode( 'my_shortcode', 'my_shortcode' );
   ```

8. Style the Plugin: Use CSS to adjust and match the plugin's design to the current website's branding, typography, and overall aesthetic.

9. Test and Debug: Test the plugin thoroughly, both in the development environment and in a staging environment, ensuring it's fully compatible with the latest version of WordPress.

10. Document and Maintain: Provide comprehensive documentation, including guidelines, user manuals, and a changelog for future maintenance, updates, and support.

In conclusion, creating a custom WordPress plugin requires a detailed analysis of the website's requirements, considering existing design and functionality. Plan and develop with attention to detail, ensure compatibility with WordPress standards, tailor the design to match the website's branding, and provide comprehensive documentation for maintenance and future updates.

